1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:06,288
Ten past eleven at night, a buyer on Malika's site types a card and presses Send code.

2
00:00:06,338 --> 00:00:12,312
Malika's shop accepts only some card networks, and this card belongs to another one.

3
00:00:12,362 --> 00:00:14,448
The buyer's phone stays dark.

4
00:00:14,498 --> 00:00:20,256
No code is sent, no network is asked, and the form answers at once.

5
00:00:20,306 --> 00:00:25,520
How did the card zone know, before asking anyone?

6
00:00:25,570 --> 00:00:32,480
In Lesson 3, the masked number kept its first six digits, because they say who gave out the card.

7
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:35,240
Tonight, those digits do the work.

8
00:00:35,290 --> 00:00:40,256
A card's first digits name the card network and the bank that issued the card.

9
00:00:40,306 --> 00:00:44,672
They are called the BIN, short for bank identification number.

10
00:00:44,722 --> 00:00:51,968
Each network publishes its own list of BINs: which first digits belong to which bank.

11
00:00:52,018 --> 00:00:54,824
That is the network's BIN list.

12
00:00:54,874 --> 00:00:59,000
The card zone copies those lists in through the adapters.

13
00:00:59,050 --> 00:01:03,848
It copies them when it starts, and again on the bank's chosen schedule.

14
00:01:03,898 --> 00:01:08,648
Sometimes a network does not answer, or answers with an empty list.

15
00:01:08,698 --> 00:01:11,528
Then the card zone keeps its old list.

16
00:01:11,578 --> 00:01:14,336
An old list is better than no list.

17
00:01:14,386 --> 00:01:19,544
The lookup is like a phone's country code, where a longer code is more exact.

18
00:01:19,594 --> 00:01:22,544
The card zone takes the first eight digits.

19
00:01:22,594 --> 00:01:25,880
It tries all eight, then seven, then six.

20
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:28,064
The longest match wins.

21
00:01:28,114 --> 00:01:34,040
The lookup runs in memory, so it is fast and never touches the database.

22
00:01:34,090 --> 00:01:39,152
And the part that knows BINs only ever sees those first eight digits.

23
00:01:39,202 --> 00:01:41,312
It never sees the whole number.

24
00:01:41,362 --> 00:01:45,536
A BIN nobody has listed is not an error.

25
00:01:45,586 --> 00:01:48,080
The answer is simply an empty list.

26
00:01:48,130 --> 00:01:50,072
Now the order that matters.

27
00:01:50,122 --> 00:01:57,656
Before any network is asked to send a code to a buyer's phone, the card zone checks the card's network this way.

28
00:01:57,706 --> 00:02:02,528
If the shop does not take that network, the card is refused right there.

29
00:02:02,578 --> 00:02:04,544
No network is bothered.

30
00:02:04,594 --> 00:02:06,416
No phone buzzes.

31
00:02:06,466 --> 00:02:12,176
I think this is the cheapest safety in the whole system: the request you never send.

32
00:02:12,226 --> 00:02:17,048
Skip the check, and the card zone asks the wrong centre to send a code.

33
00:02:17,098 --> 00:02:21,368
A real person's phone buzzes for a payment that can never work.

34
00:02:21,418 --> 00:02:24,896
A network carries a request it should never see.

35
00:02:24,946 --> 00:02:27,080
Tonight's buyer saw all of it.

36
00:02:27,130 --> 00:02:33,104
Above the card form, the bank's payment page lists the networks Malika's shop accepts.

37
00:02:33,154 --> 00:02:37,280
The buyer typed a card from another network and pressed Send code.

38
00:02:37,330 --> 00:02:41,096
The form said: "This shop doesn't accept this card.

39
00:02:41,146 --> 00:02:43,904
Use another card." Nothing was sent.

40
00:02:43,954 --> 00:02:49,880
Then the buyer mistyped a number, and its first digits belonged to no listed network.

41
00:02:49,930 --> 00:02:54,296
The form said that no network issues cards starting with those digits.

42
00:02:54,346 --> 00:02:56,528
Again, nothing was sent.

43
00:02:56,578 --> 00:03:00,920
On the third try, the buyer used a card the shop accepts.

44
00:03:00,970 --> 00:03:03,344
A code arrived on the buyer's phone.

45
00:03:03,394 --> 00:03:08,024
The buyer typed it and confirmed, and the form said "Card accepted".

46
00:03:08,074 --> 00:03:13,904
The payment page showed the masked number, the card's network, and the name of the bank that issued it.

47
00:03:13,954 --> 00:03:17,072
That bank name came from the first digits alone.

48
00:03:17,122 --> 00:03:19,688
At the bank, nobody was woken.

49
00:03:19,738 --> 00:03:24,368
A wrong network card is a normal refusal, not an incident.

50
00:03:24,418 --> 00:03:28,952
Timur's team only hears about it if the lists stop arriving.

51
00:03:29,002 --> 00:03:34,640
Eight digits named a network and a bank, and never the person holding the card.

52
00:03:34,690 --> 00:03:42,536
To recap: the first eight digits name the network and the bank, and the card zone checks them before asking anyone.

53
00:03:42,586 --> 00:03:48,520
What does the card zone do when the shop does not take that network?

54
00:03:48,570 --> 00:03:52,240
It refuses the card right there, and sends nothing.

55
00:03:52,290 --> 00:03:57,040
Next: how the card form protects itself in the buyer's browser.
